{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>They shall not be removed from it.</b> The reason Scripture needed to say <b>from it</b> and did not suffice with saying ״they shall not be removed,״ the matter being self-understood that it means from it — it is possible that it intended to say that they need not be so tightly fastened that they would not shift at all from one end to the other within the rings themselves, but rather that they not be removed from the ark — that is the requirement. However, in Yoma (72a) they raised the question, and this is their language: Rabbi Yosi bar Chanina raised the contradiction: it is written ״in the rings of the ark the poles shall be; they shall not be removed from it,״ and it is written ״and its poles shall be brought in״ — how so? They are loosened but not pulled out; end quote. From the words of the Gemara it is inferred that ״they shall not be removed״ alone conveys to him that they not shift, and perhaps the word <b>from it</b> carries another meaning, for it comes to include that they not shift back and forth; therefore the verse says (Exodus 25:14) <b>and you shall bring.</b>" "<b>Further,</b> it appears that if it had not said ״and you shall bring,״ I would have said that what the verse said — ״from it״ — comes to say that there is no liability of lashes except for their outright removal, yet there is always a prohibition even for shifting them. Therefore Scripture says <b>and you shall bring</b> — meaning the language of bringing, for if the verse's intent were that they be fixed and unmoving, it should have said ״and you shall fix״ or ״and you shall join,״ and not ״and you shall bring,״ which would convey that they are inserted and withdrawn." "<b>And I saw</b> that the Tosafot raised the difficulty of why Rabbi Yosi bar Chanina cited the verse ״and it shall be brought in,״ and they further raised a difficulty from the verse ״and they shall place its poles״ (Numbers 4:5–7) at the time of dismantling the encampments, which appears to show that they would put them in at every encampment — from this you learn that they removed them, yet the Torah said ״they shall not be removed from it.״ And they said that there were eight rings on the ark and four poles, and the verse is precise in saying ״and you shall cast for it four rings״ etc. (Exodus 25:12), and it is written ״and two rings״ etc. — from this you learn that they are eight; examine their words there. And I do not endorse these things, for the difficulties on the letters are numerous, and the matter is simple: an error fell into the books, and one must say in place of ״and it shall be brought in״ — ״and you shall bring the poles through the rings.״" "And the difficulty from ״and they shall place its poles״ (Numbers 4:7) — it appears to me to say as follows: since the poles were for carrying the ark upon them, therefore when HaShem commanded to cover the ark with the screen-curtain, He commanded that the priests prepare the poles by extending the ends of the poles. And the meaning of ״and they shall place״ is like the Old French *confonnier* (to arrange), so that it would be in proper order and manner for the Levites to carry them on their shoulders, so that the Levites would not need to extend the ends of the poles and thereby gaze at the sacred objects. And Rabbi Yehudah of Orleans explained that they would place the poles on the shoulders of the Levites, but the Tosafot already rejected his words; examine their words there (Yoma 72a). And there are further difficulties on his words, of blessed memory, here and there, and it is more fitting to choose our interpretation. As for the reason it says ״and two rings,״ I do not know what they would explain in the verse (Exodus 28:3) ״and they shall make the garments of Aaron״ etc. ״and these are the garments,״ and necessarily one must say that there are no more than eight, and such cases are numerous, and this would be one of them, with an extra vav that comes for some homiletical exposition. Or perhaps, when HaShem commanded to make two rings that would be fixed in the body of the ark and not move, and afterward he would make two rings and insert them into the rings fixed in the ark — and these latter move freely — and this is for the sake of beauty, done with the intention of honoring those who carry, for honor and splendor. But in any event there were only two poles by which the ark was carried."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {3 "The Old French gloss קונפוני״ר is rendered following the common identification with *confonnier* (to arrange/prepare). The final paragraph contains a partially unclear argument from Exodus 28:3 whose connection to the eight-ring question is not fully transparent in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:32.452619648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}